REDUCEME regression tests
=========================

These tests run REDUCEME programs on small synthetic data and compare their
standard output and output files with reference results obtained with
REDUCEME 5.1 (git tag 5.1). They are meant to detect any change in behaviour
while the code is being modernised.

Requirements: python3 (standard library only). The programs must be built.
The references depend on the compiler options: build with the default
FCFLAGS (-g -O2) or keep -O2 when setting FCFLAGS (e.g. "-g -O2 -Wall");
without optimisation the floating-point rounding changes and some cases fail.

Running
-------

    make check                          # from the top build directory
    python3 tests/run_tests.py          # all cases
    python3 tests/run_tests.py irevx    # selected cases
    python3 tests/run_tests.py --keep   # keep the temporary directories

Each case runs in a new temporary directory that contains the synthetic
input files created by mkdata.py (img_a.u, img_ae.u, img_b.u, img_be.u,
spec.u, spece.u, xsec.u). The PGPLOT device is forced to /NULL and
reduceme_dir points to the source directory.

Adding a case
-------------

1. Write cases/<name>.case (see the format in run_tests.py). The easiest way
   to find the answers expected by a program is to run it by hand in a
   directory with the synthetic data (python3 tests/mkdata.py DIR).
2. Create its reference results with binaries built from the reference
   version (tag 5.1), never with modified code:

       python3 tests/run_tests.py --update <name>

3. Check the files in references/<name>/ (stdout.txt must not end with a
   Fortran runtime error) and commit them together with the case.

Exceptions: when a bug of version 5.1 is fixed, the affected reference
files are regenerated with the fixed code, checking that nothing else
changes; they are listed here:

- skysubm/skye.u: sky errors from the approximated formulae (they were
  written outside the image, so 5.1 wrote a frame of zeros).
- miderot/stdout.txt: without simulations <Xoff> and the final table of
  velocities were NaN (0/0); now <Xoff>=Xoff, rms=0 and the final table
  in km/s.
- leefits_new/stdout.txt, leefits_new/back.u: the missing EXTEND keyword
  left a FITSIO error status that made the OBJECT keyword "not found";
  the object name is now read.
- movel (all files): the logarithmic scale was fixed (NP lost the last
  pixel by rounding, NF1 was computed from the centre of channel NC1, and
  LOGSCALE mishandled the last pixel and pixels ending on a linear
  border), so all results change slightly.
- miderot/stdout.txt, midez/stdout.txt, midez/midez.txt: the filter is
  now computed for the FFT size (NSIZE instead of NSIZE/2), so the range
  of Ki and the offsets/velocities change slightly.
- fitshead, outfitshead: both stopped with a gfortran runtime error in 5.1
  (writing into the output file after reading it to the end); their
  references were created with the fixed code and checked by hand.
- adnhand: it stopped with "End of record" in 5.1 (list-directed output
  into a CHARACTER*15 variable); the reference was created with the
  fixed code and its output files checked against an independent
  computation.

Comparison rules
----------------

- stdout and ASCII files: numbers within a relative tolerance (default 1e-6,
  configurable per case with "rtol" and "atol"); the rest must be identical.
  The version banner is removed and absolute paths are replaced by
  $reduceme_dir and $workdir. Upper limits of 1000 or more in the prompts
  "(N1,...,N2)" are replaced by "<limit>" in the output and in the
  reference: they derive from the maximum image dimensions, 2400 in the
  5.1 build used for the references and 65536 now.
- FITS files: header cards with the date and the HISTORY card with the
  REDUCEME version are ignored; data must be identical. The same date
  cards copied into text files (outfitshead) are not compared either.
- REDUCEME (binary) files: byte by byte.
